# Which diagnostic codes are allowed to be WARNINGS. Hand-maintained,
# and that is the entire point: everything else in this directory is
# written by the compiler, so an invariant read off a golden is an
# invariant the compiler under test decides.
#
# It was not hypothetical. An adversary flipped one token in
# `typecheck.ax` - `SEV_ERROR` to `SEV_WARNING` on AX3004 - re-blessed
# the AXDL goldens from the resulting compiler, and this gate passed:
# 55 checks, 0 failed, every span still verified. That compiler exits 0
# on a type-incorrect program and emits LLVM for it, including a call
# through an `inttoptr` of an integer. A guaranteed segfault, shipped as
# a successful build, and green.
#
# Re-run with this file in place, in a shadow tree so the real one was
# never touched: the re-bless still succeeds - it has to, it is only
# writing down what the compiler said - and the gate then exits 1 with
#
#     FAIL severity: AX3004 renders as a WARNING in a golden but is
#     not in tests/diagnostics/severity.policy
#
# which is the drill this file exists to pass.
#
# The reason the gate could not see it is that the severity letter in
# the golden and the exit status are the SAME field: `countErrorDiags`
# and `sevSigil` both read `(memGetWord d 0)`, which docs/diagnostics.md
# records as the design. So "the golden says E, therefore the run exits
# non-zero" is a theorem about the compiler's source rather than a test
# of it. This file breaks the circle by being the one artifact here that
# no compiler writes.
#
# One code per line. A code absent from this list must never appear as
# `W` in any `.axdl` golden.
#
# AX3010 - PROMOTED TO AN ERROR 2026-08-25, and no longer listed here.
#          It had been excused as "a claim that is merely wrong about
#          code that compiles", which is true of the CODE and beside
#          the point: the tag is a claim the author WROTE, and a build
#          that ships a false one has published a guarantee it does not
#          keep. The two objections docs/agent-harness.md 6 recorded
#          are both gone - the over-approximating shapes now route to
#          AX3037, and `symbols` prints its table for a file that fails
#          to compile. What remains warning-shaped is the UNCHECKABLE
#          half, and that is AX3037/AX3038 below, which stay.
# AX3037 - an AXTAG claim the effect walk cannot check, because the
#          body calls a value it could not resolve. A limit of the
#          ANALYSIS rather than a defect in the program - the code
#          compiles and may well be correct - so it informs and does
#          not refuse. AX3010 one line up USED to share this reasoning
#          and no longer does, which is this list's rule working rather
#          than an exception to it: the shapes the walk could not check
#          were routed HERE, and once AX3010 fired only where the walk
#          had an answer there was nothing left excusing it. The
#          standing risk is the same one stated precisely - this list
#          is for the unanswerable, and a code that becomes answerable
#          leaves it.
AX3037
# AX3038 - a `handle` whose body reaches a call the walk cannot
#          resolve, so the handled-effects list cannot be checked.
#          AX3011 - the same question ANSWERED - keeps SEV_ERROR at
#          every site it fires. This is the unanswerable half, and
#          `(handle ((b.f) 7) (Alloc) ...)` over a pure `b.f` is a
#          correct program: refusing it would make dispatch through a
#          stored function unwriteable rather than unverified.
AX3038
# AX3039 - an AXTAG key one edit from `pure` or `effect`. The key
#          namespace is open by decision, so an unknown key is not an
#          error and this does not make it one; it reports the shape
#          that costs something, where a typo means the claim was
#          never checked.
AX3039
# AX3040 - PROMOTED TO AN ERROR 2026-08-25, and no longer listed here.
#          It is left as a comment because this file's whole subject is
#          which codes may be warnings, and a code that stopped being
#          one is exactly the fact the next reader will come looking
#          for.
#
#          It stayed a warning because the rule conflated two
#          signatures and only one of them is unsound:
#
#            (:: conjure (-> Int a))     ; body CASTS a word out
#            (:: panic   (-> String a))  ; body NEVER RETURNS
#
#          Measured 2026-08-25 on the tree before the change: both
#          check clean with the identical AX3040, then `conjure` exits
#          139 and `panic` runs correctly and exits 70. Promoting it as
#          it stood would have refused the second, and the help would
#          have told that author to tag a diverging function
#          `;@axiom:raw` - which is not a raw word read, and which
#          pollutes the one enumeration the tag exists to keep exact.
#
#          "What promotion needs first is a way to tell divergence from
#          a cast" - the previous text of this block, and of
#          `tests/selfhost/976-diverging-tyvar.ax`. That is what was
#          built. A function whose result is a type variable no
#          parameter mentions can produce it only by a `cast` or by a
#          call to another such function, so the set splits by a
#          fixpoint over tail positions: assume they all diverge, then
#          strike out any whose tail can produce a value. What survives
#          is not reported at all; what is struck out is this error.
#          `347-result-only-tyvar.ax` is the struck-out control and
#          `tests/selfhost/976-diverging-tyvar.ax` is the surviving
#          one - it draws NOTHING now, which is what that file said
#          would happen if anything ever learned the difference, so it
#          moved to the corpus that can hold a fixture with no
#          diagnostic. `352-tyvar-delegation.ax` keeps the silent
#          controls here, beside three errors.
#
#          The code grew a SECOND ARM the same day, and it is an error
#          from the start rather than a warning that waited: the rule
#          asked whether the variable appears in a PARAMETER, which
#          files a variable inside a function-typed parameter under
#          "witnessed" and reported nothing at all for
#          `(:: apply1 (-> (-> a Int) Int))` - measured, check OK, exit
#          139. The spine is split by VARIANCE now, and there is no
#          warning stage for it because the question that made the
#          first arm wait does not arise: `apply1` returns an `Int`
#          perfectly well and fabricates on the way, so there is no
#          diverging reading to confuse with a cast.
#          `353-callback-tyvar.ax` is that arm's fixture, with the
#          higher-order control every ordinary callback has.

# AX3051 - a `restrict(no-io | no-alloc | no-foreign)` claim over a body
#          the effect walk could not close, in one of the two directions
#          it cannot: the row is a LOWER bound (a call it did not resolve)
#          and the effect is absent from it, or the row is an UPPER bound
#          (a function named without being called, a trait method with
#          more than one implementation) and the effect is present in
#          it. A limit of the ANALYSIS rather than a defect in the
#          program: `(fn (viaField b n) ((b.f) n))` over a pure `b.f` is
#          a correct program, and refusing it would make dispatch through
#          a stored function unwriteable under a restriction rather than
#          unverified. This is AX3037's argument at one remove - same
#          readings (`effPartial` for the lower bound, `effPossible`
#          for an effect the body may perform; the row-global `effByRef`
#          they replaced excused a definite effect beside a possible
#          one, and every caller of a `{hole}`-printing function read
#          as unverifiable), same corpus files carrying the marks
#          (`docs/reference.md`, AXTAG Keys), same standing risk:
#          this list is for the unanswerable,
#          and a code that becomes answerable leaves it. The answerable
#          half is AX3049 - a restriction the walk refuted - which is an
#          error from its first day, because the objections that held
#          AX3010 back are already gone, and the effect either is in the
#          row or is not. AX3052, a name that is not a restriction, is an
#          error too: the list is closed, so an unknown name is a claim
#          and not metadata.
AX3051
# AX3048 `deprecated-name` - a reference to a name its own declaration
#          marks `;@axiom:deprecated(...)`. A warning by DESIGN and not
#          as a staging step, which is the whole difference between
#          this and the four above. The name still works and still
#          compiles: refusing it here would make deprecation and
#          removal the same event, and separating those two is the
#          only thing a deprecation notice is for.
#
#          It is what makes the notice enforceable at the other end.
#          `scripts/check-compat.sh` permits a REMOVAL outright when
#          the previous release's baseline carried `#deprecated=`
#          (`docs/compatibility.md` COMPAT-7), so the warning is the
#          half a caller sees and the baseline is the half the gate
#          sees. Promoting this to an error would strand every caller
#          in the release that announces the change, which is the one
#          release in which their code must still build.
AX3048
